  editorialNote: "The seven journeys, thirty-nine reading sections, visual models, and the diagnostic are editorial learning aids. The sections follow Ghazali's own order: his preface and opening on silence, then the twenty banes as he numbers them. The longer banes are divided into several consecutive sections, and backbiting into eight, without adding or moving anything. The seven-part speech atlas groups those banes for navigation; it is not Ghazali's own division. The English is an original rendering made from the public Arabic text, not a translation and not a substitute for one. Reports and inherited stories are presented as material Ghazali transmitted; this edition does not grade every narration. Where the book gives rulings, such as when a lie is allowed, who may be cursed, and when naming a fault is not backbiting, they are Ghazali's as a Shafii jurist states them. They are a reading of one scholar, not a source to act from; ask someone qualified in your own school. The diagnostic applies Ghazali's first tests to one thing a reader says, and cannot pronounce on it.",
